"""English — details, prophets_a (58 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_033": "The verse belongs to the account of Adam's creation near the end of Surah Sad: \"When your Lord said to the angels, 'I am going to create a human being from clay'\" (38:71). The next verse completes it: \"When I have fashioned him and breathed into him of My spirit, then fall down to him in prostration\" (38:72). All the angels prostrated, except Iblis, who was proud (38:73-74). A Muslim remembers that the first human was formed from plain earth and honoured with a spirit Allah breathed into him.",
"prophets_034": "The Qur'an records the whisper itself: \"Your Lord did not forbid you this tree except that you become angels or become of the immortals\" (7:20). Shaytan then swore to them, \"I am to you a sincere adviser\" (7:21), and when they tasted the tree their nakedness appeared and they began covering themselves with leaves of Paradise (7:22). Their Lord reminded them that He had forbidden that tree and had told them Shaytan was a clear enemy (7:22). A Muslim learns that temptation arrives dressed as friendly advice with a false promise attached.",
"prophets_035": "The verse gives the whole exchange: \"He said, 'What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you?' He said, 'I am better than him. You created me from fire and created him from clay'\" (7:12). Just before, Allah had said to the angels, \"Prostrate to Adam,\" and all prostrated except Iblis (7:11). The answer came at once: \"Descend from it; it is not for you to be arrogant in it. Go out, you are of the debased\" (7:13). A Muslim learns that the first sin recorded in the Qur'an was pride in one's origin.",
"prophets_036": "The verse reads: \"Then Allah sent a crow scratching in the ground to show him how to hide the body of his brother. He said, 'Woe to me! Have I failed to be like this crow and hide the body of my brother?' And he became of the regretful\" (5:31). The two sons, unnamed in the Qur'an, had each offered a sacrifice; it was accepted from one only, and the slain brother had refused to raise his hand in return, saying, \"I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (5:28). A Muslim learns from the verse that follows that killing one soul unjustly is like killing all mankind (5:32).",
"prophets_037": "Surah Maryam introduces him with the words it uses for Ibrahim and Musa: \"And mention in the Book Idris. He was a man of truth and a prophet. And We raised him to a high station\" (19:56-57), the raising being verse 19:57. The surah then gathers these prophets in one verse: those on whom Allah bestowed favour, who fell down prostrating and weeping when the verses of the Most Merciful were recited to them (19:58). Elsewhere Idris is named among the patient (21:85). A Muslim learns that truthfulness and patience are what Allah recorded about this prophet.",
"prophets_038": "Surah Hud sets the scene on the water: \"It sailed with them through waves like mountains, and Nuh called to his son, who was apart: 'O my son, come aboard with us and do not be with the disbelievers.' He said, 'I will take refuge on a mountain that will protect me from the water.' Nuh said, 'There is no protector today from the command of Allah except for whom He gives mercy'\" (11:42-43). Nuh then pleaded, \"My son is of my family\" (11:45), and was told he was not, for his conduct was not righteous (11:46). A Muslim learns that being a prophet's child saves no one; only faith does.",
"prophets_039": "The end of the flood is told in one verse: \"And it was said, 'O earth, swallow your water, and O sky, withhold.' And the water subsided, the matter was accomplished, and it came to rest on al-Judi. And it was said, 'Away with the wrongdoing people'\" (11:44). This follows the drowning of Nuh's son, who had refused to board (11:42-43). Nuh was then told, \"Disembark in security from Us and blessings upon you\" (11:48). A Muslim sees in this verse earth and sky obeying a single command, and the believers settling in peace where Allah chose.",
"prophets_040": "Surah Hud gives the command at the moment the flood began: \"When Our command came and the oven overflowed, We said, 'Load into it of each kind two mates, and your family, except those against whom the word has gone forth, and whoever has believed.' But none had believed with him except a few\" (11:40). Nuh had built the ship \"under Our eyes and Our inspiration\" (11:37), and as they boarded he said, \"In the name of Allah is its sailing and its anchoring\" (11:41). A Muslim learns that Allah preserved every creature in pairs together with the few believers.",
"prophets_041": "The verse reads: \"And he constructed the ship, and whenever an assembly of the eminent of his people passed by him, they ridiculed him. He said, 'If you ridicule us, then we will ridicule you just as you ridicule'\" (11:38). He added that they would soon know who receives a disgracing punishment (11:39). Nuh was building on dry land, after Allah had told him that none of his people would believe except those who already had (11:36), so the mockers saw a ship with no water in sight. A Muslim learns to keep doing what Allah commands, calmly, even when the powerful laugh.",
"prophets_042": "Surah Al-Isra opens with the Night Journey of the Prophet ﷺ (17:1), turns to the Book given to Musa (17:2), then addresses the Children of Israel through their ancestor: \"O descendants of those We carried with Nuh. Indeed, he was a grateful servant\" (17:3). Those spoken to are reminded that they exist because their forefathers were carried in the ark, and that the man who led them there is remembered by Allah for one quality above all. A Muslim learns that gratitude is the mark Allah set beside the name of Nuh, who preached so long and was saved.",
"prophets_043": "Surah Al-Haqqa describes their end in detail: \"As for 'Ad, they were destroyed by a screaming, violent wind, which He imposed upon them for seven nights and eight days in succession, so you would see the people there fallen as if they were hollow trunks of palm trees\" (69:6-7). The surah then asks, \"Do you see any remains of them?\" (69:8). The Qur'an also records what 'Ad used to say: \"Who is greater than us in strength?\" (41:15). A Muslim learns that a people proud of their strength were removed by moving air, commanded for exactly eight days.",
"prophets_044": "The verse continues: \"And the blast seized those who had wronged, and they became within their homes fallen prone, as if they had never lived there\" (11:67-68). Salih had presented them the she-camel of Allah as a sign and warned, \"Do not touch her with harm, lest a near punishment seize you\" (11:64). They hamstrung her, and Salih said, \"Enjoy yourselves in your homes for three days; that is a promise not to be belied\" (11:65). A Muslim learns that a sign from Allah is to be respected, and that a prophet's warning is fulfilled to the day.",
"prophets_045": "The verse reads: \"And when Ibrahim said to his father Azar, 'Do you take idols as gods? Indeed, I see you and your people in manifest error'\" (6:74). Right after, the Qur'an says, \"Thus did We show Ibrahim the realm of the heavens and the earth, that he would be among the certain\" (6:75), and then tells of his looking at the star, the moon and the sun (6:76-78). In Surah Maryam each of his words to his father begins \"O my father\" (19:42-45). A Muslim learns from Ibrahim to speak the truth to family plainly, yet with tenderness.",
"prophets_046": "The verse reads: \"So he made them into fragments, except a large one among them, that they might return to it\" (21:58). Ibrahim had promised, \"By Allah, I will surely plan against your idols after you have turned away\" (21:57). When his people asked who had done this to their gods (21:59), he was brought before them and said, \"Rather, this one, the largest of them, did it, so ask them, if they can speak\" (21:63). They turned to themselves and admitted, \"You are the wrongdoers\" (21:64). A Muslim learns from Ibrahim to make people see the truth for themselves.",
"prophets_047": "The three verses trace his reasoning: \"When the night covered him, he saw a star. He said, 'This is my lord.' But when it set, he said, 'I do not love those that set'\" (6:76). When the moon set, he said, \"If my Lord does not guide me, I will surely be among the people gone astray\" (6:77). When the sun set too, he told his people, \"I am free of what you associate with Allah\" (6:78), and turned his face to Him who created the heavens and the earth (6:79). A Muslim learns that whatever rises and disappears cannot be worshipped; only the Creator remains.",
"prophets_048": "Surah Al-Baqara gives the full request: \"And when Ibrahim said, 'My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.' He said, 'Have you not believed?' He said, 'Yes, but so that my heart may be at rest.' He said, 'Take four birds and draw them to yourself; then put a portion of them on each hill; then call them, they will come to you in haste'\" (2:260). The verse before tells of a man made dead for a hundred years and then raised (2:259). A Muslim learns from Ibrahim that asking to see is not doubt when it comes from a heart that already believes.",
"prophets_049": "Surah Hud records the visit: \"Our messengers came to Ibrahim with good tidings; they said, 'Peace.' He said, 'Peace,' and did not delay in bringing a roasted calf\" (11:69). When he saw that their hands did not reach for it, he grew wary, and they told him, \"Do not fear; we have been sent to the people of Lut\" (11:70). Surah Adh-Dhariyat adds that he went quietly to his family, brought a fat calf, placed it before them and asked, \"Will you not eat?\" (51:26-27). A Muslim learns from Ibrahim to return a greeting of peace and to serve a guest quickly, unasked.",
"prophets_050": "The verse reads: \"And his wife was standing, and she laughed. Then We gave her good tidings of Ishaq, and after Ishaq, Ya'qub\" (11:71). She answered, \"Woe to me! Shall I give birth while I am an old woman and this, my husband, is an old man?\" (11:72), and the angels replied, \"Are you amazed at the command of Allah? The mercy of Allah and His blessings be upon you, people of the house\" (11:73). The news came in the same visit that announced the punishment of Lut's people (11:70). A Muslim learns that Allah's gift can arrive long after a person has stopped expecting it.",
"prophets_051": "Surah As-Saffat tells it from the father's side: \"When he reached with him the age of striving, he said, 'O my son, I have seen in a dream that I sacrifice you, so see what you think'\" (37:102). The son, unnamed in these verses, answered that his father would find him, if Allah willed, among the steadfast (37:102). \"When they had both submitted and he laid him down on his forehead, We called to him, 'O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision'\" (37:103-105). A Muslim learns from father and son that submission is \"if Allah wills\" on the lips and steadiness in the heart.",
"prophets_052": "The verse in full: \"Our Lord, I have settled some of my descendants in a valley without cultivation near Your Sacred House, our Lord, that they may establish the prayer. So make hearts among the people incline toward them and provide them with fruits, that they may be grateful\" (14:37). The prayer had begun, \"My Lord, make this city secure and keep me and my sons away from worshipping idols\" (14:35), and it ends with thanks for Isma'il and Ishaq (14:39). A Muslim notices the order of Ibrahim's asking: first that his family pray, then that people love them, then food.",
"prophets_053": "The verse continues with Allah's reply: \"And when Ibrahim said, 'My Lord, make this a secure city and provide its people with fruits, whoever of them believes in Allah and the Last Day.' He said, 'And whoever disbelieves, I will grant him enjoyment for a little; then I will force him to the punishment of the Fire'\" (2:126). Just before, Allah had made the House \"a place of return for the people and a place of security\" (2:125). A Muslim learns that Ibrahim asked provision for believers, while Allah's provision in this world reaches everyone; the difference appears in the Hereafter.",
"prophets_054": "The prayer is the last of three spoken at the building of the House: \"Our Lord, and send among them a messenger from themselves who will recite to them Your verses and teach them the Book and wisdom and purify them\" (2:129). Before it, \"when Ibrahim was raising the foundations of the House, and Isma'il,\" they said, \"Our Lord, accept from us\" (2:127). The Qur'an later describes the sending of Muhammad ﷺ in these very words: a messenger from among them, reciting His verses, purifying them, teaching the Book and wisdom (62:2). A Muslim sees the Prophet ﷺ as the answer to a prayer made beside the Ka'ba.",
"prophets_055": "Surah An-Nahl gives several qualities in one breath: \"Indeed, Ibrahim was a nation, devoutly obedient to Allah, inclining toward truth, and he was not of those who associate others with Allah; grateful for His favours, He chose him and guided him to a straight path\" (16:120-121). Allah gave him good in this world and a place among the righteous in the Hereafter (16:122). Then comes the command to the Prophet ﷺ: \"Follow the religion of Ibrahim, inclining toward truth\" (16:123). A Muslim learns that one person alone on the truth can weigh as much as a whole community.",
"prophets_056": "Surah Maryam continues past the verse: \"And mention in the Book Isma'il. Indeed, he was true to his promise, and he was a messenger and a prophet. And he used to enjoin on his people prayer and zakat, and he was pleasing to his Lord\" (19:54-55). The surah names Isma'il in his own right, as a prophet with a message and a people. Elsewhere the Qur'an shows him raising the foundations of the House with his father and praying, \"Our Lord, accept from us\" (2:127). A Muslim learns that keeping one's word is a quality Allah praises by name.",
"prophets_057": "Surah Hud describes the morning: \"When Our command came, We made the highest part of the city its lowest and rained upon it stones of hard clay, layered, marked from your Lord\" (11:82-83). The night before, the angels had told Lut to leave with his family in the last part of the night, letting none look back except his wife, who would be struck with the rest, and had said, \"Their appointed time is the morning. Is not the morning near?\" (11:81). A Muslim learns that the punishment was measured and marked, and came at the hour the angels named.",
"prophets_058": "The verse reads: \"Allah presents an example of those who disbelieved: the wife of Nuh and the wife of Lut. They were under two of Our righteous servants but betrayed them, so those two did not avail them from Allah at all, and it was said, 'Enter the Fire with those who enter'\" (66:10). The next two verses give the opposite example, believing women in houses of disbelief: the wife of Pharaoh, and Maryam, daughter of 'Imran (66:11-12). A Muslim learns that each soul answers for its own faith, and that living beside a prophet does not replace believing in him.",
"prophets_059": "Ya'qub is named before his birth in the good news to Ibrahim's wife: \"Then We gave her good tidings of Ishaq, and after Ishaq, Ya'qub\" (11:71). The Qur'an keeps the line together: \"And We gave him Ishaq and Ya'qub in addition, and all of them We made righteous\" (21:72). Yusuf in prison said, \"I have followed the religion of my fathers, Ibrahim and Ishaq and Ya'qub\" (12:38). A Muslim learns that one faith passed from Ibrahim to his son Ishaq, to his grandson Ya'qub, and on to Yusuf.",
"prophets_060": "The verse reads: \"And he turned away from them and said, 'Oh, my sorrow over Yusuf,' and his eyes became white from grief, for he was holding it in\" (12:84). His sons had just returned from Egypt without their younger brother, saying, \"Your son has stolen\" (12:81). They told their father he would go on remembering Yusuf until he fell ill or died (12:85), and he answered, \"I only complain of my suffering and my grief to Allah\" (12:86). A Muslim learns that grief may be deep and still be carried to Allah alone, with hope intact.",
"prophets_061": "When Yusuf had made himself known, he said: \"Take this shirt of mine and cast it over the face of my father; he will become seeing. And bring me your whole family\" (12:93). As the caravan left Egypt, Ya'qub said, \"I find the smell of Yusuf, if you do not think me feeble-minded\" (12:94). Then \"when the bearer of good news arrived, he cast it over his face, and he returned to seeing,\" and Ya'qub said, \"Did I not tell you that I know from Allah what you do not know?\" (12:96). A Muslim learns that the relief Ya'qub hoped for came from the very son he had wept over.",
"prophets_062": "The verse is a prayer: \"My Lord, prison is dearer to me than that to which they invite me. And if You do not turn their plot away from me, I might incline toward them and be of the ignorant\" (12:33), and \"his Lord answered him and turned their plot away from him\" (12:34). Just before, the women of the city had cut their hands at the sight of him, and the wife of al-'Aziz had declared that if he did not obey her he would be imprisoned (12:31-32). A Muslim learns from Yusuf to choose hardship over sin, and to ask Allah for protection rather than trust his own strength.",
"prophets_063": "The verse reads: \"Two young men entered the prison with him. One of them said, 'I saw myself pressing wine.' The other said, 'I saw myself carrying bread on my head, from which the birds were eating. Tell us its interpretation; we see that you are a doer of good'\" (12:36). Before answering, Yusuf spoke to them about the one God and the religion of his fathers (12:37-40). Then he told them: one would serve his master wine, the other would be crucified (12:41). A Muslim learns from Yusuf to put the call to Allah before every other service.",
"prophets_064": "Yusuf's answer gave both a reading and a plan: \"You will sow for seven years as usual, and what you harvest leave in its ears, except a little from which you eat. Then will come after that seven hard years which will eat up what you stored for them, except a little you keep\" (12:47-48). He added that a year of rain and pressing would follow (12:49). The king had dreamt of seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain and others dry (12:43). A Muslim learns from Yusuf that true knowledge serves people: he told them what to do.",
"prophets_065": "The verse reads: \"He said, 'Appoint me over the storehouses of the land; indeed, I am a knowing guardian'\" (12:55). The king had said, \"Bring him to me; I will appoint him exclusively for myself,\" and after speaking with him, \"You are today established and trusted with us\" (12:54). The next verse explains: \"Thus We established Yusuf in the land, to settle in it wherever he willed\" (12:56). A Muslim learns that Yusuf asked for the post where his honesty and knowledge would protect people through the famine, and that Allah counted it among his good deeds.",
"prophets_066": "The verse reads: \"When he had furnished them with their supplies, he put the drinking cup into the bag of his brother. Then a crier called out, 'O caravan, you are thieves'\" (12:70). Yusuf had already taken his brother aside and told him, \"I am your brother, so do not grieve over what they used to do\" (12:69). The brothers themselves named the penalty, that whoever's bag it was found in would be held (12:75), and the Qur'an then says, \"Thus did We plan for Yusuf\" (12:76). A Muslim learns that Allah's arrangement worked through Yusuf's wisdom to bring the family together.",
"prophets_067": "The verse gives the advice and its limit: \"O my sons, do not enter through one gate, but enter through different gates. And I cannot avail you against Allah at all. The decision is only for Allah; upon Him I have relied\" (12:67). The next verse says that entering as their father instructed availed them nothing against Allah, but satisfied a need in Ya'qub's soul, \"and he was a possessor of knowledge because of what We had taught him\" (12:68). A Muslim learns from Ya'qub to take sensible precautions while placing all trust in Allah.",
"prophets_068": "The verse reads: \"And the brothers of Yusuf came and entered upon him; and he recognised them, but they did not know him\" (12:58). The years of plenty had passed as Yusuf advised, and the hard years had begun (12:47-48). Having given them their measure, he said, \"Bring me a brother of yours from your father. Do you not see that I give full measure and that I am the best of hosts?\" (12:59). The boy thrown into the well now stood over the men who did it, and served them. A Muslim learns from Yusuf that power is a chance to do good, not to settle scores.",
"prophets_069": "The verse reads: \"And the family of Pharaoh picked him up, that he might become to them an enemy and a grief. Indeed, Pharaoh and Haman and their soldiers were sinners\" (28:8). Allah had inspired Musa's mother: \"Suckle him; but when you fear for him, cast him into the river and do not fear and do not grieve. We will return him to you and make him one of the messengers\" (28:7). Pharaoh's wife then said, \"A comfort of the eye for me and for you. Do not kill him\" (28:9). A Muslim learns that no plan stands against Allah's: the hunted child was raised in the hunter's house.",
"prophets_070": "The verses read: \"And We had forbidden to him all wet nurses before, so she said, 'Shall I direct you to a household that will take care of him for you and be sincere to him?' So We returned him to his mother, that her eye might be comforted and she might not grieve, and that she might know that the promise of Allah is true\" (28:12-13). The sister was there because her mother had told her, \"Follow him,\" and she watched from a distance unnoticed (28:11). A Muslim learns that the promise of 28:7 was kept exactly, through a baby's refusal and a girl's quick word.",
"prophets_071": "The verse reads: \"He entered the city while its people were unaware and found two men fighting, one of his own people and one of his enemies. The one of his people called him for help against his enemy, so Musa struck him and killed him. He said, 'This is of the work of Shaytan; he is a clear, misleading enemy'\" (28:15). At once he prayed, \"My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me,\" and Allah forgave him (28:16). A Muslim learns from Musa to name a wrong for what it is and turn to Allah without delay.",
"prophets_072": "The verse reads: \"And a man came running from the farthest end of the city. He said, 'O Musa, the chiefs are conferring about you to kill you, so leave; I am one of your sincere advisers'\" (28:20). The Qur'an does not name him. Musa left the city fearful and watchful, praying, \"My Lord, save me from the wrongdoing people\" (28:21), and as he set out toward Madyan he said, \"It may be that my Lord will guide me to the right way\" (28:22). A Muslim learns that a sincere warning is a mercy, and that leaving in fear can still be done with trust in Allah.",
"prophets_073": "The verses read: \"He found a crowd of people watering, and apart from them two women holding back their flock. He said, 'What is the matter with you?' They said, 'We cannot water until the shepherds move away, and our father is an old man.' So he watered for them, then turned back to the shade and said, 'My Lord, I am in need of whatever good You send down to me'\" (28:23-24). One of the two then came walking shyly to say that her father invited him (28:25). A Muslim learns from Musa to help the weak unasked, then turn to Allah with his own need.",
"prophets_074": "The verse reads: \"I wish to marry you to one of these two daughters of mine, on condition that you work for me eight years; and if you complete ten, that is from you. I do not want to make it hard for you. You will find me, if Allah wills, of the righteous\" (28:27). The Qur'an does not name the old man. One daughter had said, \"O my father, hire him; the best you can hire is the strong and the trustworthy\" (28:26). Musa accepted, adding, \"Allah is witness over what we say\" (28:28). A Muslim learns that a fair agreement is stated clearly, made with ease, and sealed with Allah as witness.",
"prophets_075": "The verse reads: \"Indeed, I am your Lord, so remove your sandals; you are in the sacred valley of Tuwa\" (20:12). Musa had seen a fire and told his family to wait while he fetched a brand or found guidance at it (20:10); when he came near, he was called by name (20:11). What follows is the first message he received: \"I have chosen you, so listen to what is revealed. Indeed, I am Allah; there is no god but Me, so worship Me and establish the prayer for My remembrance\" (20:13-14). A Muslim learns that Musa's mission began with the oneness of Allah and the prayer.",
"prophets_076": "The verse reads: \"And draw your hand to your side; it will come out white, without disease, another sign, that We may show you some of Our greatest signs\" (20:22-23). It follows the first sign: asked \"What is that in your right hand, O Musa?\" he described his staff and its everyday uses (20:17-18); told to throw it, he saw it become a moving snake and was told, \"Take it and do not fear\" (20:19-21). Then came the command: \"Go to Pharaoh; indeed, he has transgressed\" (20:24). A Muslim learns that both signs came from ordinary things, a shepherd's staff and his own hand.",
"prophets_077": "The four verses form one prayer: \"My Lord, expand for me my chest, and ease for me my task, and untie the knot from my tongue, that they may understand my speech\" (20:25-28). Musa said it immediately after being told, \"Go to Pharaoh; indeed, he has transgressed\" (20:24). He went on to ask for his brother Harun as a helper, \"that we may glorify You much and remember You much\" (20:29-34), and Allah answered, \"You have been granted your request, O Musa\" (20:36). A Muslim learns from Musa to ask, before any hard task, for an open heart and clear words.",
"prophets_078": "The verses read: \"And appoint for me a helper from my family, Harun, my brother. Strengthen me through him and let him share my task, that we may glorify You much and remember You much\" (20:29-34). Allah replied, \"You have been granted your request, O Musa\" (20:36). In Surah Al-Qasas Musa gives his reason: \"My brother Harun is more fluent than me in speech, so send him with me as a support to confirm me\" (28:34). The two were sent together, told to speak to Pharaoh with gentle words (20:43-44). A Muslim learns that asking for help is part of doing a task well.",
"prophets_079": "The verses read: \"And We inspired Musa, 'Throw your staff,' and at once it swallowed up what they were faking. So the truth was established, and what they were doing came to nothing. They were defeated there and turned back humbled. And the magicians fell down in prostration\" (7:117-120). These were men who had asked Pharaoh for a reward if they won (7:113-114). Pharaoh threatened to cut off their hands and feet and crucify them (7:124); they answered, \"To our Lord we will return\" (7:125). A Muslim learns that those who knew the craft best were the first to see that this was no craft.",
"prophets_080": "The verse reads: \"So We sent upon them the flood and locusts and lice and frogs and blood, as distinct signs, but they were arrogant and were a criminal people\" (7:133). Before this, Allah had seized Pharaoh's people with years of drought and shortage of fruits so that they might take heed (7:130). Each time a plague fell they said, \"O Musa, call on your Lord for us; if you remove this, we will believe you\" (7:134), and each time it was lifted they broke their word (7:135). A Muslim learns that signs alone do not change a heart that has decided not to see.",
"prophets_081": "The verse reads: \"So today We will save you in body, that you may be a sign to those who come after you. And indeed, many among the people are heedless of Our signs\" (10:92). The Children of Israel had crossed the sea, and Pharaoh pursued them \"in tyranny and enmity\" until, as he drowned, he said, \"I believe that there is no god except the One in whom the Children of Israel believe\" (10:90). The answer was, \"Now? When you disobeyed before and were of the corrupters?\" (10:91). A Muslim learns that faith declared only when death is certain is not accepted.",
"prophets_082": "The verse reads: \"And Allah presents an example of those who believed: the wife of Pharaoh, when she said, 'My Lord, build for me near You a house in Paradise, and save me from Pharaoh and his deeds, and save me from the wrongdoing people'\" (66:11). The Qur'an does not give her name. She is set against the wives of Nuh and Lut (66:10) and beside Maryam (66:12), and earlier she was the one who said of the infant Musa, \"Do not kill him\" (28:9). A Muslim notices that she asked first for nearness, \"near You\", and only then for the house.",
"prophets_083": "The verse reads: \"And We appointed for Musa thirty nights and completed them with ten, so the term of his Lord was completed as forty nights. And Musa said to his brother Harun, 'Take my place among my people, do right, and do not follow the way of the corrupters'\" (7:142). Surah Al-Baqara mentions the same forty nights and what happened during them: \"then you took the calf after him\" (2:51). Musa came back angry and grieved, and threw down the Tablets (7:150). A Muslim learns that Musa did not leave his people without a guide, and that a people's test often comes while their teacher is away.",
"prophets_084": "The verse reads: \"He said, 'My Lord, show me, that I may look at You.' He said, 'You will not see Me, but look at the mountain; if it remains in its place, then you will see Me.' But when his Lord revealed Himself to the mountain, He made it level, and Musa fell unconscious. When he recovered, he said, 'Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance, and I am the first of the believers'\" (7:143). The next verse answers him: \"O Musa, I have chosen you over the people with My messages and My words\" (7:144). A Muslim learns from Musa that awe before Allah ends in glorifying Him and in thanks.",
"prophets_085": "The verse reads: \"And We wrote for him on the Tablets of all things, an admonition and explanation for all things: 'Take them with firmness and order your people to take the best of it'\" (7:145). This came at the meeting where Allah told Musa He had chosen him with His messages and His words (7:144). When Musa returned and found his people with the calf, he threw the Tablets down in anger (7:150), and when the anger subsided he took them up again, \"and in their inscription was guidance and mercy\" (7:154). A Muslim learns that what Allah writes is to be held firmly and acted on at its best.",
"prophets_086": "Allah told Musa on the mount: \"We have tested your people after you, and as-Samiri has led them astray\" (20:85). Musa returned angry and grieved (20:86), and his people said they had thrown in loads of the people's ornaments, \"and thus did as-Samiri throw\" (20:87). \"And he brought out for them a calf, a body with a lowing sound, and they said, 'This is your god and the god of Musa'\" (20:88). Harun had already told them, \"You are only being tested by it; your Lord is the Most Merciful, so follow me\" (20:90). A Muslim learns that a crowd can be led into plain falsehood by one man, and that the warner among them was right.",
"prophets_087": "The verse reads: \"And when Musa prayed for water for his people, We said, 'Strike the rock with your staff.' And twelve springs gushed forth from it; every people knew its drinking place. 'Eat and drink from the provision of Allah, and do not go about the earth spreading corruption'\" (2:60). Surah Al-A'raf tells the same event and explains the number: Allah had divided them into twelve tribes, and each tribe knew its own spring (7:160). A Muslim learns that provision from Allah comes joined to a command not to spread corruption on the earth.",
"prophets_088": "The verse reads: \"And We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails: 'Eat of the good things We have provided you.' And they did not wrong Us, but they were wronging themselves\" (2:57). It comes among the favours listed to the Children of Israel: the rescue from Pharaoh (2:49-50) and the forty nights (2:51). Later they asked for herbs, cucumbers, garlic, lentils and onions, and were asked, \"Would you exchange what is better for what is less?\" (2:61). A Muslim learns that food sent from the sky was still met with complaint, and that gratitude is what Allah asks for.",
"prophets_089": "The verse reads: \"They said, 'Call upon your Lord to make clear to us what her colour is.' He said, 'He says it is a yellow cow, bright in colour, pleasing to those who look'\" (2:69). Musa had told them, \"Allah commands you to slaughter a cow,\" and they asked whether he was mocking them (2:67). They asked what kind: neither old nor young (2:68); after the colour they asked again, and were told one not trained for ploughing or watering, without blemish (2:70-71). \"So they slaughtered her, though they almost did not\" (2:71). A Muslim learns that a simple command grows heavy when met with endless questions.",
"prophets_090": "The passage begins: \"Qarun was from the people of Musa, but he oppressed them. And We gave him treasures whose keys would weigh down a band of strong men\" (28:76). His people told him not to exult and to seek the Hereafter with what Allah had given him (28:77); he answered, \"I was only given it because of knowledge I have\" (28:78). \"So We caused the earth to swallow him and his home, and he had no company to help him against Allah\" (28:81). A Muslim learns that wealth attributed to oneself rather than to Allah is a burden, not a gift.",
}
